{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>In the wadi of Gerar.</b> Far from the city. \"And he returned and dug the wells of water which they had dug in the days of Abraham his father, and the Philistines had stopped them up\" — before Isaac departed from Gerar he went back and dug them. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 26:18). And it appears from his words that they are the wells mentioned there [earlier], as it says (Genesis 26:15), \"the Philistines stopped them up and filled them with dirt.\" But this is not correct, for how would they grant him permission when they had said, \"they are a hindrance to us because of the troops\"? Rather, they are different wells in a different place, for \"the wadi of Gerar\" is a place name, or the wadi extends from Gerar to another land. And when the Philistines envied him — they are the officers who were in Gerar, the king's province — they stopped up the wells that were his by inheritance from his father within the boundary of the city of Gerar. And the king sent him away from the city of his throne's residence, and he went to another city, which perhaps is not even under his sovereignty, though it is in the land of the Philistines. And there were other wells that Abraham had dug, since he had dwelt in that place for many days. And when Abraham died and Isaac did not dwell there, the Philistines, the inhabitants of the land, stopped them up — not out of hatred toward him, but in the days of Abraham they had not wanted to stop them up out of respect for him, for they said, perhaps he will return to dwell in the land. Therefore Isaac returned and dug them in the wadi. And the herdsmen of the wadi of Gerar contended with him, saying, \"The water is ours.\" And the reason for \"saying\" (לאמר): behold, the well is in the wadi, and the waters of the wadi are what accumulate there, and from their drainage the well fills and the wadi diminishes because of them — and so behold, they are ours. Therefore Scripture mentions, \"and they found there a well of living water,\" to say that it was a spring gushing living water — its waters were not from the wadi, as his contenders had claimed."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {0 "The word וימצו (\"they accumulate\") is understood here from context as referring to the wadi waters gathering into the well; the subsequent word ומתמציתם (\"from their drainage/seepage\") is translated literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:35:35.922761978Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}